A","title":"Decision"},{"id":"B","title":"Context"},{"id":"C","title":"Options"},{"id":"D","title":"Consequences"}],"text":"Decision span=3 Any .md file whose frontmatter has a crux: key joins the graph, wherever it is. The value is the kind. File names and folders carry no meaning, except that a code kind's folder is its default code: path and an enclosing container is its default parent. Context span=3 - Teams keep docs in different places; a naming convention would fight every existing layout. - A service's description belongs next to its code, where agents and reviewers already look. - The previous model needed one architecture.yaml ; it grew large and drifted from the code. Options span=3 Option Pros Cons Fit --- --- --- --- A — Header key crux: anywhere No convention. Files live next to code. Must walk the repo. ok B — Fixed folders ( docs/system/… ) Cheap to find. Far from code; a second tree to keep in sync. no C — One YAML map One file to read. Grows unreadable; merge conflicts; no prose. no Consequences span=3 - Positive: files sit next to the code; code: and parents need no typing. - Cost: every load walks the repo; .gitignore , hidden and vendor folders are skipped and only the frontmatter is read. - Follow-up: architecture.yaml stays optional for hand-drawn layouts."},"source":"---\ntitle: Discover crux files by their header, not by name or folder\nstatus: Accepted\ndate: 2026-10-06\nauthor: abtin\ntags: [format, discovery]\ncomponents: [graph]\napplies_to: [internal/adr/**]\nreopen_when: [\"header scan takes more than 1 s on a 10k-file repo\"]\n---\n\n## Decision {span=3}\n```callout ok Decision\nAny `.md` file whose frontmatter has a `crux:` key joins the graph, wherever it is. The value is the kind. File names and folders carry no meaning, except that a code kind's folder is its default `code:` path and an enclosing container is its default parent.\n```\n\n## Context {span=3}\n- Teams keep docs in different places; a naming convention would fight every existing layout.\n- A service's description belongs next to its code, where agents and reviewers already look.\n- The previous model needed one `architecture.yaml`; it grew large and drifted from the code.\n\n## Options {span=3}\n| Option | Pros | Cons | Fit |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A — Header key `crux:` anywhere | No convention. Files live next to code. | Must walk the repo. | ok |\n| B — Fixed folders (`docs/system/…`) | Cheap to find. | Far from code; a second tree to keep in sync. | no |\n| C — One YAML map | One file to read. | Grows unreadable; merge conflicts; no prose. | no |\n\n## Consequences {span=3}\n- Positive: files sit next to the code; `code:` and parents need no typing.\n- Cost: every load walks the repo; `.gitignore`, hidden and vendor folders are skipped and only the frontmatter is read.\n- Follow-up: `architecture.yaml` stays optional for hand-drawn layouts.\n"}